{"name":"Chilli Pepper","hex":"#ac1e3a","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chilli pepper","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of chili pepper."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chilli_pepper"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chilli Pepper (entertainer)","blocks":[{"text":"Chilli Pepper was an entertainer, female impersonator and trans woman who was known as a frequent talk show guest in the 1980s and was a HIV/AIDS awareness advocate. Pepper's name was added to the National LGBTQ Wall of Honor in 2025."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chilli_Pepper_(entertainer)"},{"title":"Chili pepper","blocks":[{"text":"Chili peppers, also spelled chile or chilli, are varieties of berry-fruit plants from the genus Capsicum, which are members of the nightshade family Solanaceae, cultivated for their pungency. They are used as a spice to add pungency in many cuisines. Capsaicin and the related capsaicinoids give chili peppers their intensity when ingested or applied topically. Chili peppers exhibit a range of heat and flavors. This diversity is the reason behind the availability of different types of chili powder, each offering its own taste and heat level."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Madame_Jeanette_and_other_chillies.jpg/330px-Madame_Jeanette_and_other_chillies.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chili_pepper"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Leiden_University_Library_-_Seikei_Zusetsu_vol._25%2C_page_019_-_%E8%95%83%E6%A4%92_-_Capsicum_annuum_L.%2C_1804_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Leiden_University_Library_-_Seikei_Zusetsu_vol._25%2C_page_019_-_%E8%95%83%E6%A4%92_-_Capsicum_annuum_L.%2C_1804_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Leiden_University_